No, not now. After the second one, they knew it was a human being, although it acts like an animal.

01:03:47.990 --> 01:03:53.590
They can make tests. Jenny, who works at the hospital, said the third one was really horrible.

01:03:53.590 --> 01:03:55.860
Sooner they get him locked up, the better.

01:04:00.120 --> 01:04:01.540
I'm terribly sorry, I thought...

01:04:01.540 --> 01:04:04.440
Third was another one last night.

01:04:05.270 --> 01:04:08.370
I'm terribly sorry to upset you, I didn't think.

01:04:09.260 --> 01:04:11.040
But there's no need to worry.

01:04:11.560 --> 01:04:14.320
He only attacks soldiers in uniform and they're all armed now.

01:04:16.060 --> 01:04:20.700
Last night, I'll pour you another cup of coffee.

01:04:23.010 --> 01:04:23.810
I'd better stay.

01:04:24.730 --> 01:04:26.110
Would you like me to stay with you, Miss Ellie?

01:04:26.770 --> 01:04:28.530
No, it's all right.

01:04:29.020 --> 01:04:30.000
It's all right, it's...

01:04:30.380 --> 01:04:30.840
I'm shocked.

01:06:36.720 --> 01:06:49.140
he's got out again that's such a wonderful dream out he's gone out on who's

01:06:49.140 --> 01:07:00.000
gone Joyce he's moved the bench away and climbed out do you remember that bench far away

01:07:03.710 --> 01:07:13.840
do you remember? Joyce, you must listen to me. Listen to me. He's escaped. Just as well.

01:07:13.840 --> 01:07:23.670
They cooped up there. It doesn't matter. But there's been another murder. Another soldier.

01:07:23.670 --> 01:07:28.810
We've got to tell the police now. We've got to tell them the whole thing. Why? Because

01:07:28.810 --> 01:07:30.000
he could kill somebody else.

01:07:30.920 --> 01:07:35.480
Suppose it was Alan next time. It could be. We've got to talk now.

01:07:36.470 --> 01:07:39.370
You're very fond of your young corporal, aren't you, Ellie?

01:07:39.730 --> 01:07:43.530
I suppose you think it makes up.

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:48.060
Is that what you think, Ellie? Is it?

01:07:48.220 --> 01:07:50.910
I've never seen you like this before. What's wrong?

01:07:52.020 --> 01:07:58.740
No, nothing's wrong. I feel fine.

01:08:00.280 --> 01:08:05.190
Who's been able to rely on you before?

01:08:05.190 --> 01:08:09.640
What am I going to do, Joyce? Tell me! Tell me what to do!

01:08:49.770 --> 01:08:53.190
I'm the mistress of the dark on TV!

01:08:53.190 --> 01:08:56.000
I'm a big star, you know?

01:08:56.000 --> 01:08:59.000
Anyway, I'm just calling folks up to remind them to watch movie macabre.

01:08:59.000 --> 01:09:00.000
And me, of course.

01:09:00.090 --> 01:09:06.190
- Of course. Hello? - Hello to you, sister.

01:09:06.190 --> 01:09:11.650
- Ahem. - Actually, I think I know what Ellie was about to do.

01:09:11.650 --> 01:09:15.540
I think she was calling up that Barbara Woodhouse thing with the Obedience School, you know.

01:09:15.540 --> 01:09:20.600
- Whoopies! - I've seen that woman at work before, and she is really amazing!

01:09:20.600 --> 01:09:24.930
Quiet, won't take her five minutes to have that beast beaten out of her leg.

01:09:24.930 --> 01:09:29.220
What a good beast! What a good beast!

01:09:31.210 --> 01:09:34.800
How long has it been since you've spent a nice romantic evening together?

01:10:03.320 --> 01:10:05.030
Okay, lads, bring 'round the back, please.

01:10:05.030 --> 01:10:26.470
Oh, shed 'round the corner.

01:11:04.640 --> 01:11:05.540
Major Mortimer?

01:11:05.540 --> 01:11:08.200
Ah, this is Chief Superintendent Paddock here.

01:11:08.200 --> 01:11:10.790
Yes, the bells have gone off.

01:11:10.790 --> 01:11:12.680
Close them all.

01:11:12.680 --> 01:11:15.920
All men indeed.

01:11:15.920 --> 01:11:19.310
Oh, and I'm going to need three men for the house here.

01:11:19.310 --> 01:11:23.020
Ah, two to be posted at the Shed in the Garden,

01:11:23.020 --> 01:11:25.520
and one man inside the house.

01:11:25.520 --> 01:11:28.610
Well, I don't think he'll come back here, but...

01:11:30.030 --> 01:11:31.330
They've got a storm, you might.

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:33.670
Fine, thanks.

01:11:34.140 --> 01:11:35.700
Yes, I'll keep in touch.

01:11:36.610 --> 01:11:37.090
All right, goodbye.

01:11:41.340 --> 01:11:41.860
Miss Valentine?

01:11:44.930 --> 01:11:46.000
Miss Valentine?

01:11:47.450 --> 01:11:51.570
I've got to tell them now, Joyce.

01:11:52.330 --> 01:11:53.910
You do understand, don't you?

01:11:53.910 --> 01:11:55.070
I've got to.

01:11:56.120 --> 01:11:59.230
If you think it's best, Ellen, whatever you say.

01:12:36.520 --> 01:12:45.360
that's you tell us about it starting young here will write it all down that all right a long time

01:12:45.360 --> 01:13:00.000
ago there's so many things to explain you just take it down tell us about it in your own world

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:29.760
And it was all to do with daddy, father. He was a wonderful man then. I can still remember him like that. Only just, baby. He was tall and handsome when he went away to the war. That was right at the very beginning. He was just mother and Joyce and me. I can remember that the house seemed so empty to me.

01:13:30.920 --> 01:13:45.570
We all missed him so much. And when he came home on me, he looked beautiful in his uniform, and the house was full of laughter and it was sunny again just like before he went away.

01:13:45.570 --> 01:13:59.350
He took George and me to the fairground that time. We went on the roundabout and they helped us carefully. We had a fine time. He bought us some ice cream from the Italian man.

01:14:02.170 --> 01:14:03.010
She didn't like it.

01:14:03.010 --> 01:14:06.340
It was so different in those days.

01:14:07.760 --> 01:14:10.740
And then suddenly he was gone.

01:14:13.030 --> 01:14:15.270
We didn't go to the station to see him off.

01:14:16.420 --> 01:14:17.550
Just mother.

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:21.030
She cried a lot when she came home.

01:14:22.480 --> 01:14:26.770
Told us that Daddy had gone to France to fight in the war.

01:14:27.610 --> 01:14:30.000
We didn't understand much about it at the time.

01:14:31.150 --> 01:14:37.520
We cried because she cried, because we missed him so much.

01:14:40.390 --> 01:14:45.790
He came back once more, to look older, more tired.

01:14:48.110 --> 01:14:49.680
It was still fun, mind you.

01:14:50.600 --> 01:14:55.950
It was winter that time, and the lake of little meal was frozen over, solid.

01:14:56.770 --> 01:15:00.000
We're scapegoat, and Daddy made us a toboggan.

01:15:00.760 --> 01:15:08.570
real mother, carpentry. He loved it. He could make anything. And he pulled us right to the

01:15:08.570 --> 01:15:13.670
top of Deep Dean Hill on the top of office. And then he left us home with screams and

01:15:13.670 --> 01:15:21.690
screams and it was marvelous. Now Deep Dean Hill's gone too. That's where they built those

01:15:21.690 --> 01:15:30.000
new houses. Then daddy went back to France and we used to read the newspapers and mother

01:15:31.850 --> 01:15:34.400
He always said, how's Muppet and Muppet?

01:15:34.860 --> 01:15:37.630
He called Joyce with him as Muppet when she was born.

01:15:38.050 --> 01:15:41.050
He called me Muppet because of my hair.

01:15:41.170 --> 01:15:42.410
He said it was just like a mop.

01:15:42.410 --> 01:15:49.730
And when I was little, he used to lift me up and turn me upside down and pretend to mop the floor with me.

01:15:49.790 --> 01:15:52.560
And I used to scream and pretend to be frightened.

01:15:53.420 --> 01:15:54.770
Of course I wasn't here.

01:15:55.620 --> 01:15:57.580
Then we read about the big push.

01:15:58.520 --> 01:15:59.540
That's an awkward...

01:16:01.080 --> 01:16:06.470
kept thinking that daddy was going to be killed because we knew he was right there in the front

01:16:06.470 --> 01:16:18.320
line. I shall never forget the day that the telegram came and mother's face when she saw it. At first

01:16:18.320 --> 01:16:30.000
she didn't want to open it. Joyce said she had to and she cried and laughed and then she cried again and she said that daddy was

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:37.360
coming home. The telegram wasn't from him. It was from a friend of his, a captain, something

01:16:37.360 --> 01:16:43.510
or other. Nick told us the time of the train and everything. And we all went to the station

01:16:43.510 --> 01:16:44.210
to meet him.

01:18:06.080 --> 01:18:18.780
The mother told us that it was because of what had happened to him in France, that he was gassed and had shell shock, and that we must try and understand and be patient, and that eventually he would be better.

01:18:18.780 --> 01:18:28.050
He never was. He was never the same man again.

01:18:31.440 --> 01:18:35.780
Then in 1921, Mother told us she was expecting another baby.

01:18:36.620 --> 01:18:42.420
I don't know why, but we were rather shocked somehow, Father being like he was.

01:18:44.090 --> 01:18:45.870
Anyway, Mother had Stephen.

01:18:46.900 --> 01:18:48.880
He was a beautiful baby.

01:18:49.480 --> 01:18:51.280
Joyce and I adored him.

01:18:52.930 --> 01:18:54.510
Father didn't take to him at all.

01:18:55.800 --> 01:19:00.000
Mother wasn't well after that, and Father got worse and worse all the time.

01:19:01.340 --> 01:19:03.660
It wouldn't have Stephen in the same room with him.

01:19:05.230 --> 01:19:06.570
And then Mother died.

01:19:07.310 --> 01:19:09.690
She hadn't been there until Stephen was born.

01:19:10.950 --> 01:19:13.130
Father hated him even more after that.

01:19:13.990 --> 01:19:16.230
And one day, he started to brick up the fella.

01:19:17.850 --> 01:19:19.910
He said he was going to put Stephen in there.

01:19:20.400 --> 01:19:21.860
Let him rot, he said.

01:19:22.830 --> 01:19:25.830
But of course, Joyce and I stopped him.

01:19:27.280 --> 01:19:27.740
Wait a minute.

01:19:28.100 --> 01:19:28.620
Time out.

01:19:28.900 --> 01:19:29.380
Hold it.

01:19:30.570 --> 01:19:32.880
as to be continued, written all over it.

01:19:32.880 --> 01:19:35.310
When Ellie sits down to tell a story,

01:19:35.310 --> 01:19:38.390
she sure doesn't leave out many details, does she?

01:19:38.390 --> 01:19:40.840
She could tell you the flavor of the ice cream cone

01:19:40.840 --> 01:19:44.640
she had at the amusement park when she was only six years old.

01:19:44.640 --> 01:19:46.540
She could probably tell you the number of polka dots

01:19:46.540 --> 01:19:47.920
on her father's tie.

01:19:47.920 --> 01:19:52.440
But just look at that poor cop trying to write all this stuff down.

01:19:52.440 --> 01:19:54.000
Boy, right now, he's saying,

01:19:54.000 --> 01:19:57.080
"Why can't we have a tape recorder like they do in the state?"

01:19:57.080 --> 01:20:00.000
Looks like you're the Reader's Digest version out of this,

01:20:00.860 --> 01:20:04.960
Which brings to mind a little story of my own!

01:20:04.960 --> 01:20:07.730
Okay, there was this, um, this cannibal, see?

01:20:07.730 --> 01:20:10.950
Who came upon two big game hunters in the jungle.

01:20:10.950 --> 01:20:13.670
And they were just sitting around the campfire, see?

01:20:13.670 --> 01:20:17.870
And one of them was writing a letter, and the other one was reading a book.

01:20:17.870 --> 01:20:20.230
So, which one did the cannibal eat?

01:20:20.230 --> 01:20:26.870
Well, he ate the one who was reading a book, 'cause he knew that Reader's Digest writers cramps!

01:20:26.870 --> 01:20:27.930
What?

01:20:27.930 --> 01:20:29.890
Well...

01:20:30.480 --> 01:20:34.160
I thought that one was from 3 there. I mean, it beats the story of the hell it was telling, doesn't it?

01:20:34.730 --> 01:20:36.590
Well, back to the movie after this.

01:20:37.920 --> 01:20:42.710
But your sweet lips a little closer...

01:21:12.640 --> 01:21:15.890
It turned out that Stephen was strange in some way, did it?

01:21:19.610 --> 01:21:29.800
I never spoke about him.

01:21:30.760 --> 01:21:34.480
Steven wrote him letters, he never opened them, he just threw them on the fire without a word.

01:21:35.050 --> 01:21:39.330
Of course, we didn't tell Steven that, and so he just kept on writing.

01:21:40.390 --> 01:21:46.370
If he asked, we said that father enjoyed his letters, but that he never wrote back to anybody.

01:21:46.370 --> 01:21:53.760
And then, it must have been in 1933, daddy died.

01:21:54.650 --> 01:22:00.000
Well, he'd been in bed for over a year, so none of us missed him very much.

01:22:01.080 --> 01:22:06.640
We were quite glad, really, because we'd realized long ago that he would never get any better.

01:22:07.040 --> 01:22:09.610
So we thought this was probably the best thing.

01:22:10.590 --> 01:22:11.650
How did Stephen take it?

01:22:12.130 --> 01:22:16.290
He was heartbroken, as if he and father had been everything to each other.

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:19.060
Josh and I could never understand it.

01:22:19.330 --> 01:22:21.290
That was in 1933.

01:22:21.710 --> 01:22:27.290
Yes, we started reading the newspapers then about Hitler and Germany.

01:22:28.180 --> 01:22:29.790
One crisis after another.

01:22:30.410 --> 01:22:37.690
us. Where was Stephen then? Oh, he was still at boarding school. But Joyce said that he

01:22:37.690 --> 01:22:49.640
should come home and go to day school. She said we needed a man about the house. There

01:22:49.640 --> 01:22:56.240
was only one big row. Stephen wanted to join the OTC at school. But Joyce put her foot

01:22:56.240 --> 01:23:00.000
down firmly over that. She wrote to the headmaster and said she

01:23:00.280 --> 01:23:11.350
allow it under any circumstances. Stephen was furious. Joyce was adamant. I just wanted to look

01:23:11.350 --> 01:23:18.040
after you, make sure you were safe. After all, I brought you up since you were a baby. I've been

01:23:18.040 --> 01:23:30.000
mother and father to you. All you wanted to do was get away from me, go to the war. You do understand I

01:23:32.140 --> 01:23:32.620
Don't you?

01:23:35.450 --> 01:23:46.140
Never had it.

01:23:48.260 --> 01:23:49.360
But you are what it is.

01:24:00.830 --> 01:24:01.750
Oh, come on.

01:24:34.120 --> 01:24:39.790
He took a job there for summer. He said that if he earned some money, he could help us to manage.

01:24:40.280 --> 01:24:43.100
And that he could buy his own clothes when he went to university.

01:24:44.020 --> 01:24:46.080
It was only a casual job, mind you.

01:24:46.320 --> 01:24:47.380
And what happened after this?

01:24:47.910 --> 01:24:50.810
One day, Joyce said there was going to be another war.

01:24:51.750 --> 01:24:55.800
I was sure she was right. All the papers seemed to be full of it.

01:24:56.870 --> 01:24:59.740
Joyce said there would be conscription straight away this time.

01:25:00.640 --> 01:25:06.640
everybody who was 18 would be called up and made to go and fight in France. She kept on

01:25:06.640 --> 01:25:12.910
and on about it, about Stephen and what had happened to Daddy and about how she'd never

01:25:12.910 --> 01:25:20.700
let it happen to Stephen. Never, never. So Stephen came across from Blackpool one Sunday

01:25:20.700 --> 01:25:26.730
afternoon and said she wanted him to go to Canada about how much better the universities

01:25:26.730 --> 01:25:30.000
were there. But Stephen wouldn't go. He would determine

01:25:30.220 --> 01:25:35.770
not go. He even said that if there was a war, he wouldn't wait to be conscripted. He'd

01:25:35.770 --> 01:25:41.890
join up. He said that if Joyce had let him join the OTC at school, he'd have been able

01:25:41.890 --> 01:25:47.940
to be an officer straight away. But he didn't care. He'd join up and try and get a commission

01:25:47.940 --> 01:25:54.740
afterwards. When he'd gone back to Blackpool, Joyce was out of her mind. She couldn't leave

01:25:54.740 --> 01:26:00.000
it alone. She went on and on about what had happened to Daddy and how we could have been

01:26:00.440 --> 01:26:05.750
same thing happened to Stephen. She swore she wouldn't, and she said that I should feel

01:26:05.750 --> 01:26:11.520
the same. Stephen only wanted to be a not like his father.

01:26:11.520 --> 01:26:18.880
Is that funny? I'd never thought of it like that, but that's what it amounted to. And

01:26:18.880 --> 01:26:25.310
one day, Joyce told me that she had a plan. She talked and talked about it all through

01:26:25.310 --> 01:26:30.000
the following week. She said that it wouldn't be difficult, that we could do it.

01:26:30.250 --> 01:26:32.960
that it would be better for Stephen.

01:26:32.960 --> 01:26:37.750
Eventually I had to agree.

01:26:37.750 --> 01:26:41.910
Joyce was always stronger about things than I was.

01:26:41.910 --> 01:26:45.890
She went to the doctor and told him she couldn't sleep.

01:26:45.890 --> 01:26:51.340
He said it was her nerves and gave her some tablets.

01:26:51.340 --> 01:26:56.390
She wrote to Stephen and told him to come over on Sunday.

01:26:56.390 --> 01:26:58.770
Said that she wanted to talk to him.

01:27:00.220 --> 01:27:09.280
she made tea, and not long afterwards, Stephen fell asleep in his chair. Four tablets in his

01:27:09.280 --> 01:27:18.590
tea. The doctor said she must never have more than one. I was terrified, but she said everything

01:27:18.590 --> 01:27:30.000
would be all right, and we took him down to the cellar. We had everything ready. All the stuff was there,

01:27:31.180 --> 01:27:36.300
Because Father had started on it ages ago, when he was angry with Stephen.

01:27:38.780 --> 01:27:41.010
You picked him up in the cellar?

01:27:41.860 --> 01:27:45.020
Yes. We both thought it was best for him.

01:27:45.610 --> 01:27:48.330
You kept him there? For how long?

01:27:49.500 --> 01:27:56.380
First, he used to shout and scream, but if it was especially bad, or we thought someone was going to call,

01:27:56.460 --> 01:27:59.590
we just gave him some tablets and a drink or something.

01:28:00.250 --> 01:28:01.690
He had to drink, you see.

01:28:02.630 --> 01:28:06.750
Well, I had to be stronger and stronger as time went on.

01:28:07.330 --> 01:28:10.890
But gradually he stopped making any noise at all.

01:28:12.130 --> 01:28:14.000
Just whimper now and again.

01:28:16.780 --> 01:28:21.730
It was very difficult during the war, with only two rations for three people.

01:28:23.470 --> 01:28:25.480
And you kept him there right through the war?

01:28:26.910 --> 01:28:28.130
Yes, of course.

01:28:29.240 --> 01:28:30.000
And when it was over?

01:28:31.240 --> 01:29:00.000
the awful part we couldn't let him out then. He was too far gone. He wasn't normal. He's been in there nearly 30 years. We thought it was best for him, you see. That's what we thought. Boy, I tell you, as a movie, this would have made a horrific radio show. If you asked me, Stephen was a lot better.

01:29:00.990 --> 01:29:02.600
that cellar for all those years.

01:29:02.600 --> 01:29:07.800
You know, at least you didn't have to listen to Ellie with her long, boring stories.

01:29:07.800 --> 01:29:10.850
I've got some good news and some bad news.

01:29:10.850 --> 01:29:13.940
First, the good news: Ellie's finally run out of steam.

01:29:13.940 --> 01:29:16.060
She finished selling her little yarn.

01:29:16.060 --> 01:29:18.310
But now, here's the bad news.

01:29:18.310 --> 01:29:20.960
Now her sister, Joyce, gets to tell her version.

01:29:20.960 --> 01:29:27.590
Moving the car returns with the exciting conclusion to

01:29:27.590 --> 01:29:29.740
beep in the cellar.

01:29:33.070 --> 01:29:41.600
What do you think they'll do?

01:29:41.600 --> 01:29:45.730
Well, that's not for me to decide, Mr. Ballantyne. I just have to report.

01:29:45.730 --> 01:29:48.820
I don't think he'll come back here. We're bound to catch him soon.

01:29:48.820 --> 01:29:53.240
Have a good night's rest. Good night.

01:30:45.260 --> 01:30:50.600
They wanted someone in the house, so I sort of volunteered.

01:30:50.600 --> 01:30:52.790
It seemed better to have somebody they knew around.

01:31:00.990 --> 01:31:03.270
He gave her a light sedative, sent her off to bed.

01:31:03.270 --> 01:31:05.600
Whoever would have believed it.

01:31:05.600 --> 01:31:07.620
Two dear sweet old ladies.

01:31:07.620 --> 01:31:09.600
What the earth made them do it.

01:31:09.600 --> 01:31:11.590
Who knows what makes people do things?

01:31:11.590 --> 01:31:13.990
What do you suppose they'll do with them?

01:31:13.990 --> 01:31:15.430
I don't know.

01:31:15.430 --> 01:31:17.130
I don't think there's a lot they can do.

01:31:17.130 --> 01:31:19.590
Can't really lock up two old ladies like this.

01:31:19.590 --> 01:31:20.900
Don't think you can.

01:31:20.900 --> 01:31:22.660
Maybe put them in a home.

01:31:22.660 --> 01:31:25.670
Might as well leave them here, with their own things around them.

01:31:25.670 --> 01:31:27.970
They must have suffered a lot over the years.

01:31:27.970 --> 01:31:29.570
And him, the brother?

01:31:30.280 --> 01:31:34.860
They'll have to lock him up when they catch him. He can't be much more than an animal.

01:31:34.860 --> 01:31:39.380
Why soldiers? Why do you suppose he only attacks soldiers?

01:31:39.380 --> 01:31:44.610
I don't know. Probably in what was left of his brain he thought the army had something to do with him being locked up.

01:31:44.610 --> 01:31:50.000
I suppose over the years he got it all muddled up. Then he saw the uniform and that was enough.

01:31:50.000 --> 01:31:56.150
There. Poor old thing. Should I look in and see him as Ellie, do you think?

01:31:56.150 --> 01:31:58.150
I think he's asleep. Perhaps it'd be better not to wake her up.

01:31:58.150 --> 01:31:59.150
Maybe.

